Step-by-step SQL (replace placeholders in <>)

Notes: run these statements from Snowsight or SnowSQL. Execute USE ROLE ACCOUNTADMIN; for the integration creation steps (or a role that has the required global privilege).

0) Pick or create a place to store secrets

(optional but recommended: create a dedicated DB/schema for integration objects)

-- optional: create objects container
CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS GIT_INTEG_DB;
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS GIT_INTEG_DB.SECRETS;
USE DATABASE GIT_INTEG_DB;
USE SCHEMA GIT_INTEG_DB.SECRETS;

1) Create the SECRET containing the Bitbucket credentials

For Bitbucket public/private repos Snowflake expects USERNAME = 'x-token-auth' and PASSWORD = '<your-BITBUCKET_PAT>'. (Docs explicitly call out x-token-auth for Bitbucket.) 
Snowflake Documentation
+1

-- run as a role with CREATE SECRET privilege in this schema
CREATE OR REPLACE SECRET my_bitbucket_secret
  TYPE = PASSWORD
  USERNAME = 'x-token-auth'            -- required for Bitbucket
  PASSWORD = '<BITBUCKET_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN>'
  COMMENT = 'Bitbucket PAT for Git integration';

2) (Optional) Grant usage on the secret to the role that will manage the repo (e.g., SYSADMIN)

If your team will use a different role (SYSADMIN) to operate on the repo/Workspaces, grant it USAGE on the secret:

-- change <role_to_use> to the role you use for daily work (e.g., SYSADMIN)
GRANT USAGE ON SECRET my_bitbucket_secret TO ROLE <role_to_use>;


The secret lives in a schema and uses normal Snowflake RBAC — USAGE on DB/schema may also be needed. 
Snowflake Documentation

3) Create the API INTEGRATION that limits allowed endpoints and references the secret

Use API_PROVIDER = git_https_api and set API_ALLOWED_PREFIXES to your repo host/organization prefix. Reference the secret in ALLOWED_AUTHENTICATION_SECRETS. You must run this as ACCOUNTADMIN or a role with CREATE INTEGRATION. 
Snowflake Documentation
+1

USE ROLE ACCOUNTADMIN;  -- or a role with CREATE INTEGRATION

CREATE OR REPLACE API INTEGRATION my_bitbucket_api_integration
  API_PROVIDER = git_https_api
  -- restrict to your account/workspace; can be as specific as needed
  API_ALLOWED_PREFIXES = ('https://bitbucket.org/<your-workspace-or-account>')
  ALLOWED_AUTHENTICATION_SECRETS = (my_bitbucket_secret)
  ENABLED = TRUE
  COMMENT = 'API integration for Bitbucket (git_https_api)';


Tip: API_ALLOWED_PREFIXES should cover the HTTPS prefix Snowflake will call (e.g. https://bitbucket.org/my-workspace or https://bitbucket.org/<team>/<repo>). If you need a broader prefix (organization-level) you can set that — but keep it as narrow as practical. 
Snowflake Documentation

4) Create the GIT REPOSITORY object in Snowflake (the clone)

This creates the Git repository clone inside Snowflake and ties the ORIGIN, API integration, and the secret together. 
Snowflake Documentation

-- pick a repository name in Snowflake (identifier) and set ORIGIN to your HTTPS repo URL
CREATE OR REPLACE GIT REPOSITORY snowflake_myrepo
  API_INTEGRATION = my_bitbucket_api_integration
  GIT_CREDENTIALS = my_bitbucket_secret
  ORIGIN = 'https://bitbucket.org/<your-workspace-or-account>/<your-repo>.git'
  COMMENT = 'Clone of my Bitbucket repo for Workspaces';

5) (Optional) Verify the repo & fetch / show repository information

You can use SHOW / DESCRIBE commands or Snowsight UI to inspect the repository object.

SHOW GIT REPOSITORIES;
DESCRIBE GIT REPOSITORY snowflake_myrepo;

6) Use Snowsight Workspaces to create/sync a workspace with your repo

Open Snowsight → Workspaces.

Create a new Workspace and connect it to the existing Git repository you created. The UI will list Git repos available in the account; pick snowflake_myrepo and a branch (e.g., main).

Once connected you can: create files, pull the branch, edit .sql/.py files, commit, and push back. (Workspaces support pull/push/create workflows.) 
